\title{Resolution Floors for Cluster-Structured Pythagorean Search,\\
and the Rejection of a Twice-Gated Drift Anomaly}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We report the rejection, by directional disagreement between independent random seeds, of a
candidate few-percent deviation from randomness in a large clustered Pythagorean-type search,
and we develop the analysis-side mathematics that makes this outcome structurally predictable
rather than merely empirical.

The experiment compares a candidate hit count against a control hit count across $m$ disjoint
clusters (indexed by modulus, equivalently by hypotenuse), reports the pooled ratio
$r = \sum_i x_i / \sum_i y_i$, and attaches a nonparametric cluster-bootstrap confidence
interval. Three earlier legs, all descending from a single random stream, reported $r < 1$
(deficits of $1$--$5\%$) with intervals excluding $1$ from below. An arbiter run on a fresh,
uncontaminated seed --- band-9, $96$-bit moduli, $128$ clusters, $6\times10^{5}$ pairs per
cluster, $7.68\times10^{7}$ pairs total --- reports $r = 2598/2252 = 1.1536$ with interval
$[1.0540,\,1.2611]$ at the primary cut and $r = 40617/38594 = 1.0524$ with $[1.0051,\,1.1016]$
at the looser cut: a \emph{surplus}, excluding $1$ from \emph{above}.

We prove four groups of results. (1) A \emph{coverage-incompatibility theorem}: two disjoint
events cannot each have probability $\ge 1-\alpha$ unless $\alpha \ge 1/2$; with $s$
pairwise-incompatible runs, $s(1-\alpha)\le 1$. (2) An \emph{exact cluster-bootstrap variance
identity}: for a centred cluster vector $d$ and $n$ draws with replacement from $m$ clusters,
$m\sum_f(\sum_k d_{f(k)})^{2} = n\,m^{n} \sum_i d_i^{2}$; at $n=m$ the bootstrap variance of
the resampled total is exactly $\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^{2}$. (3) A \emph{resolution floor}:
$x_j/S - 1/m \le \rsd(x)$ for every cluster $j$, instantiating at the recorded profile to a
nonzero floor exceeding $0.0069$, against which the reported half-width $0.048$ is consistent.
(4) \emph{Unbounded hypotenuse multiplicity}: for every $k$ there is a hypotenuse with at least
$k$ ordered positive leg pairs, via $C_k = \prod_{v<k}((v+2)^{2}+1)$; combined with (3) this
yields genuine two-cluster Pythagorean families whose one-run relative resolution floor is
arbitrarily close to $1/2$. We also record a mediant envelope for pooled ratios, an
inverse-variance pooling certificate for the round's named follow-up condition, and a formal
statement of the display-truncation artefact that triggered one audit alarm. The verdict is
\emph{randomness-extended, gate rejected}.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Pythagorean triples; hypotenuse multiplicity; cluster bootstrap;
overdispersion; resolution floor; mediant inequality; inverse-variance pooling; confidence
interval coverage.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The experimental setting}

Consider a search over Pythagorean-type configurations at a fixed scale band. For each of $m$
moduli $N_1,\dots,N_m$ (the \emph{clusters}), the search samples a large number of pairs and
records how many are \emph{hits} --- configurations satisfying the relation under test. Two
streams run in parallel: a \emph{candidate} stream drawn from the structured arithmetic family
whose behaviour is in question, producing counts $x_1,\dots,x_m$; and a deliberately
structureless \emph{control} stream, producing counts $y_1,\dots,y_m$. The reported summary
statistic is the pooled ratio
\[
  r \;=\; \frac{\sum_{i=1}^m x_i}{\sum_{i=1}^m y_i},
\]
with a nonparametric \emph{cluster bootstrap} confidence interval: the $m$ clusters are
resampled i.i.d.\ uniformly with replacement, their counts re-added, and empirical quantiles of
the resampled ratio taken as the interval. Under the null hypothesis that the candidate family
is arithmetically indistinguishable from the control at this scale, $r$ concentrates at $1$.

\subsection{The anomaly and its death}

Three successive rounds reported $r$ below $1$: point estimates $0.9468$ (pilot), $0.988$
(gate leg G1), $0.9623$ (leg B), all at the looser cut, each with an interval excluding $1$.
An independence audit revealed that all three legs descended from the same random stream
(seed family $20260824$). An arbiter run was commissioned on a fresh seed ($20260825$), the
only leg uncontaminated by the shared stream: band-9, $96$-bit moduli, $m = 128$ clusters,
$6\times 10^{5}$ sampled pairs per cluster, total $7.68\times 10^{7}$ pairs, wall-clock
$5296.9$\,s.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
cut & candidate & control & $r$ & $95\%$ interval \\
\midrule
primary ($10^{-5}$) & $2598$  & $2252$  & $\mathbf{1.1536}$ & $[1.0540,\;1.2611]$ \\
looser ($10^{-6}$)  & $40617$ & $38594$ & $\mathbf{1.0524}$ & $[1.0051,\;1.1016]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Both intervals exclude $1$ \emph{from above}. The clean seed reads a $+5$ to $+15\%$ surplus
where the contaminated family read a deficit. The disagreement is \emph{directional}, and
Section~\ref{sec:signflip} shows that directional disagreement is a strictly stronger
refutation than one of magnitude: it falsifies at least one nominal coverage claim outright.
The recorded verdict is \emph{randomness-extended / gate rejected}. By symmetric skepticism the
surplus is also \emph{not} banked: it is a single run, inside a fluctuation envelope that
Sections~\ref{sec:boot}--\ref{sec:mult} show is unavoidable.

\subsection{Contribution}

We prove: a mediant envelope for pooled ratios (\S\ref{sec:mediant}); coverage incompatibility
and its multi-run form (\S\ref{sec:signflip}); an exact cluster-bootstrap variance identity
(\S\ref{sec:boot}); a resolution floor and its instantiation at the recorded profile
(\S\ref{sec:floor}); unbounded hypotenuse multiplicity and a near-$1/2$ floor
(\S\ref{sec:mult}); inverse-variance pooling certifying the follow-up condition
(\S\ref{sec:pool}); and the truncation artefact (\S\ref{sec:trunc}).

\section{The mediant envelope for pooled ratios}\label{sec:mediant}

Throughout, $s$ is a finite nonempty index set of clusters and $x,y : s \to \mathbb{R}$ are
count vectors with $y_i > 0$ for all $i \in s$.

\begin{definition}
The pooled candidate/control ratio is
$r(x,y) = \bigl(\sum_{i\in s} x_i\bigr)/\bigl(\sum_{i\in s} y_i\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Mediant upper bound]\label{lem:medup}
If $x_i/y_i \le M$ for all $i \in s$, then $r(x,y) \le M$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $y_i>0$, the hypothesis is $x_i \le M y_i$ for each $i$. Summing gives
$\sum_i x_i \le M\sum_i y_i$, and $\sum_i y_i > 0$ as a sum of positives over a nonempty set,
so division yields the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Mediant lower bound]\label{lem:medlow}
If $\mu \le x_i/y_i$ for all $i \in s$, then $\mu \le r(x,y)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Symmetric: $\mu y_i \le x_i$ for each $i$; sum and divide by $\sum_i y_i > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mediant envelope]\label{thm:envelope}
There exist clusters $\ell, u \in s$ with
\[
  \frac{x_\ell}{y_\ell} \;\le\; r(x,y) \;\le\; \frac{x_u}{y_u}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
As $s$ is finite and nonempty, $i\mapsto x_i/y_i$ attains a minimum at some $\ell$ and a maximum
at some $u$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:medlow} with $\mu = x_\ell/y_\ell$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:medup}
with $M = x_u/y_u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A pooled surplus is never an aggregation artefact]\label{cor:surplus}
If $r(x,y) > 1$ then $y_i < x_i$ for at least one $i \in s$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise $x_i/y_i \le 1$ for all $i$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:medup} with $M=1$ gives
$r(x,y)\le 1$.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:surplus} is the ``cluster structure is honest'' audit statement: the
arbiter's $r=1.1536$ cannot be a pure aggregation effect. Theorem~\ref{thm:envelope} carries the
complementary warning: a mediant is dominated by its largest-denominator terms, so a single
cluster carrying a large share of the control mass can drag $r$ to its own ratio. The pooled
ratio is a weighted median-like statistic whose sensitivity is governed by the biggest
clusters --- and \S\ref{sec:mult} shows those can be arbitrarily big.

\section{The sign-flip audit: disjoint intervals cannot both cover}\label{sec:signflip}

Fix a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ carrying the randomness the runs
consume, and let $\rho$ denote the estimand: the true limiting candidate/control ratio at this
scale band.

\begin{theorem}[Coverage incompatibility]\label{thm:cover}
Let $A,B\subseteq\Omega$ be disjoint events with $\mathbb{P}(A)\ge 1-\alpha$ and
$\mathbb{P}(B)\ge 1-\alpha$. Then $1 \le 2\alpha$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Disjointness gives $\mathbb{P}(A\cup B)=\mathbb{P}(A)+\mathbb{P}(B)\ge 2(1-\alpha)$, while
$A\cup B\subseteq\Omega$ gives $\mathbb{P}(A\cup B)\le 1$. Hence $2-2\alpha\le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:no95}
If $A$ and $B$ are disjoint with $\mathbb{P}(A)\ge 0.95$ and $\mathbb{P}(B)\ge 0.95$, a
contradiction follows.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:cover} with $\alpha=0.05$ yields $1\le 0.1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multi-run sign-partition bound]\label{thm:multi}
Let $A_1,\dots,A_s$ be pairwise disjoint measurable events with $\mathbb{P}(A_i)\ge 1-\alpha$
for each $i$. Then $s(1-\alpha)\le 1$, equivalently $\alpha \ge 1 - 1/s$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pairwise disjointness gives $\mathbb{P}(\bigcup_i A_i)=\sum_i \mathbb{P}(A_i)\ge s(1-\alpha)$,
and the union lies in $\Omega$.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Application.} The arbiter's looser-cut interval $[1.0051,\,1.1016]$ is contained in
$\{\rho\ge 1.0051\}$; the $20260824$ family's intervals lie in $(0,1]\subseteq\{\rho\le 1\}$.
These sets are disjoint, so the coverage events are disjoint, and by Corollary~\ref{cor:no95}
it is impossible that both families' nominal $95\%$ intervals cover the same $\rho$.

Three features deserve emphasis. The argument is \emph{assumption-light}: no normality, no
independence between runs, only countable additivity and $\mathbb{P}(\Omega)=1$. It is
\emph{sign-based}: a gate failing on a number can sometimes be repaired by widening intervals,
but no widening of both intervals preserves the exclusion of $1$ that made the gate fire. And
it is \emph{symmetric}: it identifies no guilty party, so nothing may be banked in either
direction.

The recorded point estimates straddle $1$ strictly: $0.9468 < 1 < 40617/38594$ and
$1 < 2598/2252$. Moreover $40617/38594 < 2598/2252$: the apparent effect \emph{shrinks} as the
cut is loosened and the counts grow, the signature of a fluctuation rather than of a
scale-stable arithmetic deviation.

\section{The cluster bootstrap, exactly}\label{sec:boot}

Any theorem intended to constrain the reported intervals must be a theorem about the resampling
scheme actually used, not a convenient surrogate. This section supplies the exact identity.

\begin{definition}[Resample]
Let $\iota$ be a finite nonempty cluster index type with $m=|\iota|$. A \emph{resample of length
$n$} is a map $f:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\iota$, interpreted as drawing $n$ clusters i.i.d.\ uniformly
with replacement. All $m^{n}$ resamples are equally likely, so expectations are sums divided by
$m^{n}$. Given counts $x:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$, the \emph{resampled total} is
$T^{*}(f)=\sum_{k=1}^{n} x_{f(k)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Centred resampled totals sum to zero]\label{lem:zero}
Let $d:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $\sum_i d_i = 0$. Then for every $n\ge 0$,
\[
  \sum_{f:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\iota}\;\sum_{k=1}^{n} d_{f(k)} \;=\; 0 .
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. For $n=0$ the outer sum has a single term, the empty sum, which is $0$. For
the step, decompose a resample of length $n+1$ as a pair $(a,f')$ with $a\in\iota$ the first
draw; then $\sum_k d = d_a + \sum_k d_{f'(k)}$. Summing over $f'$ first, the second term
contributes $0$ by the inductive hypothesis and the first contributes $m^{n} d_a$; summing over
$a$ gives $m^{n}\sum_a d_a = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cluster bootstrap second-moment identity]\label{thm:secondmoment}
Let $d:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$ with $\sum_i d_i = 0$ and let $n\ge 0$. Then
\[
  m\sum_{f:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\iota}\Bigl(\sum_{k=1}^{n} d_{f(k)}\Bigr)^{2}
  \;=\; n\,m^{\,n}\sum_{i\in\iota} d_i^{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $D=\sum_i d_i^{2}$ and $Q_n=\sum_f\bigl(\sum_k d_{f(k)}\bigr)^{2}$; the claim is
$mQ_n = n m^{n} D$. Induct on $n$; the base case $n=0$ reads $0=0$. For the step use the
first-draw decomposition $f\leftrightarrow(a,f')$ and expand
\[
  \Bigl(d_a + \sum_k d_{f'(k)}\Bigr)^{2}
  = d_a^{2} + 2 d_a\sum_k d_{f'(k)} + \Bigl(\sum_k d_{f'(k)}\Bigr)^{2}.
\]
Summing over all $(a,f')$: the first term contributes $m^{n} D$; the cross term vanishes,
since for fixed $a$ the sum over $f'$ is $2d_a\cdot 0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:zero}; the third term
does not depend on $a$ and contributes $m\,Q_n$. Hence $Q_{n+1} = m^{n} D + m Q_n$, and
multiplying by $m$ and applying the inductive hypothesis,
\[
  m Q_{n+1} = m^{n+1} D + m\,(n m^{n} D) = (n+1)\,m^{\,n+1} D. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

Dividing by $m\cdot m^{n}$: the resampled total of a centred vector has variance
$n\cdot\bigl(\sum_i d_i^{2}\bigr)/m$, the textbook ``$n$ times the population variance'',
derived here combinatorially.

\begin{theorem}[Bootstrap variance of the resampled total]\label{thm:bootvar}
Let $x:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$, $S=\sum_i x_i$, $\bar x = S/m$, $m\ge 1$. Then
\[
  \sum_{f:\{1,\dots,m\}\to\iota}\bigl(T^{*}(f)-S\bigr)^{2}
  \;=\; m^{\,m}\sum_{i\in\iota}\bigl(x_i-\bar x\bigr)^{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $d_i = x_i-\bar x$, so $\sum_i d_i = S - m\bar x = 0$. For any resample $f$ of length $m$,
$\sum_{k} d_{f(k)} = T^{*}(f) - m\bar x = T^{*}(f)-S$. Apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:secondmoment} with $n=m$ and cancel the factor $m>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Relative cluster dispersion]
\[
  \rsd(x) \;=\; \frac{\sqrt{\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^{2}}}{S},
  \qquad \bar x = \frac{S}{m},\quad S=\sum_i x_i .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}[$\rsd$ is the relative bootstrap standard error]\label{cor:isboot}
For $m\ge 1$,
\[
  \rsd(x) \;=\; \frac{1}{S}\sqrt{\frac{1}{m^{m}}
   \sum_{f:\{1,\dots,m\}\to\iota}\bigl(T^{*}(f)-S\bigr)^{2}} .
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Divide both sides of Theorem~\ref{thm:bootvar} by $m^{m}$ inside the square root.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:isboot} is methodological: without it the floor below would bound an ad-hoc
functional, and one could object that the reported intervals need not obey it.

\section{The resolution floor}\label{sec:floor}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution floor]\label{thm:floor}
Let $x:s\to\mathbb{R}$ with $S=\sum_{i\in s} x_i > 0$ and $m=|s|$. Then for every $j\in s$,
\[
  \frac{x_j}{S}-\frac{1}{m} \;\le\; \rsd(x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $\bar x = S/m$. The term $(x_j-\bar x)^{2}$ is one of the nonnegative summands of
$\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^{2}$, so $(x_j-\bar x)^{2}\le\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^{2}$. Taking square roots
and using $t\le|t|=\sqrt{t^{2}}$ gives
$x_j-\bar x \le \sqrt{\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^{2}}$. Divide by $S>0$ and note
$(x_j - S/m)/S = x_j/S - 1/m$.
\end{proof}

If one cluster carries a share $f=x_j/S$, the one-run relative bootstrap standard error is at
least $f-1/m$. Since the bootstrap resamples \emph{clusters}, no amount of additional sampling
\emph{within} clusters reduces it: more pairs per $N$ enlarge every $x_i$ but leave the shares,
and hence the floor, essentially unchanged.

\begin{corollary}[Dominant cluster]
If $x_j \ge (1-\delta)S$ for some $j$, then $\rsd(x)\ge 1-\delta-1/m$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$x_j/S\ge 1-\delta$; combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:floor}.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{The recorded profile.} The arbiter's cluster profile at the looser cut had $m=128$
clusters, a maximum cluster of $600$ hits (next $561$ and $540$, against a control maximum of
$359$), and grand total $40617$. Idealise it as $x_0 = 600$ and $x_i = 40017/127$ for
$1\le i\le 127$, so $\sum_i x_i = 40617$.

\begin{proposition}[The recorded run against its own floor]\label{prop:recorded}
\[
  \rsd(x)\;\ge\;\frac{600}{40617}-\frac{1}{128}\;>\;0.0069,
  \qquad
  2\times 0.0069 \;\le\; \frac{1.1016-1.0051}{2}\approx 0.048 .
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The first inequality is Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} at $j=0$, with
$600/40617\approx 0.014772$ and $1/128 = 0.0078125$, whose difference is $\approx 0.006960$.
The second is arithmetic.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:recorded} discharges the audit concern that the reported interval might be
\emph{narrower} than the cluster structure permits: the reported half-width comfortably exceeds
twice the structural floor. With a $\pm 5$--$15\%$ envelope, no single run of
$\sim 7.7\times 10^{7}$ pairs at this profile can resolve a few-percent deviation.

\section{Overdispersion is intrinsic to Pythagorean arithmetic}\label{sec:mult}

\begin{definition}[Hypotenuse cluster]
For $c\in\mathbb{N}$ let
\[
  H(c)=\bigl\{(a,b)\in\mathbb{N}^{2}\;:\;1\le a\le c,\;1\le b\le c,\;a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}\bigr\},
\]
the set of \emph{ordered} pairs of positive legs with hypotenuse $c$; $|H(c)|$ is the
\emph{hypotenuse multiplicity} of $c$.
\end{definition}

Every element of $H(c)$ is a Pythagorean triple in the usual sense, and
$H(5)=\{(3,4),(4,3)\}$, so $|H(5)|=2$.

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded hypotenuse multiplicity]\label{thm:mult}
For every $k\in\mathbb{N}$ there exists $c>0$ with $|H(c)|\ge k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For any integer $\mu\ge 2$ one has $(\mu^{2}-1)^{2}+(2\mu)^{2}=(\mu^{2}+1)^{2}$. Reindex with
$\mu=v+2$ and write
\[
  L(v)=v^{2}+4v+3\;(=\mu^{2}-1), \qquad h(v)=v^{2}+4v+5\;(=\mu^{2}+1),
\]
so $L(v)^{2}+\bigl(2(v+2)\bigr)^{2}=h(v)^{2}$ identically and $h(v)=L(v)+2$.

The map $v\mapsto h(v)$ is injective: $h(a)=h(b)$ gives $a^{2}+4a=b^{2}+4b$, i.e.\
$(a-b)(a+b+4)=0$ over $\mathbb{Z}$, and the second factor is strictly positive.

Set $C=C_k=\prod_{v=0}^{k-1} h(v)=\prod_{v=0}^{k-1}\bigl((v+2)^{2}+1\bigr)>0$. Each $h(v)$
divides $C$, so $t_v := C/h(v)$ is a positive integer with $h(v)t_v = C$. Define
$\Phi(v)=\bigl(L(v)t_v,\;2(v+2)t_v\bigr)$.

\emph{$\Phi(v)\in H(C)$.} Both coordinates are positive. Both are $\le C$: indeed
$L(v)\le h(v)$, and $2(v+2)\le h(v)$ since $(v+2)^{2}+1-2(v+2)=(v+1)^{2}\ge 0$; multiply by
$t_v$ and use $h(v)t_v = C$. Finally
\[
  \bigl(L(v)t_v\bigr)^{2}+\bigl(2(v+2)t_v\bigr)^{2}
  = h(v)^{2}t_v^{2} = \bigl(h(v)t_v\bigr)^{2} = C^{2}.
\]

\emph{$\Phi$ is injective on $\{0,\dots,k-1\}$.} Suppose $\Phi(a)=\Phi(b)$ with $a\ne b$, and let
$A=L(a)t_a=L(b)t_b$. Multiplying by the respective hypotenuses,
$A h(a) = L(a) C$ and $A h(b) = L(b) C$. Subtracting and using $h(v)=L(v)+2$,
\[
  A\bigl(h(a)-h(b)\bigr) = C\bigl(L(a)-L(b)\bigr) = C\bigl(h(a)-h(b)\bigr),
\]
so $(A-C)\bigl(h(a)-h(b)\bigr)=0$. By injectivity of $h$ the second factor is nonzero, so
$A=C$. But $L(a)<h(a)$ strictly, whence $A = L(a)t_a < h(a)t_a = C$, a contradiction.

Therefore $|H(C)|\ge k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The function $c\mapsto|H(c)|$ is unbounded.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:mult} with $k=B+1$ for any candidate bound $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The bound is far from sharp]
The construction certifies $k$ hits at $C_k$ but typically delivers many more: for $k=3$,
$C_3 = 5\cdot 10\cdot 17 = 850$ and $|H(850)| = 14$. The number of representations of $c^{2}$ as
an ordered sum of two positive squares is multiplicative in the primes $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$
dividing $c$, whereas the scaled family sees only one primitive triple per factor.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Near-half resolution floor from genuine Pythagorean clusters]\label{thm:half}
For every $\varepsilon>0$ there exist distinct hypotenuses $c_1\ne c_2$ such that the two-cluster
count vector $x=\bigl(|H(c_1)|,|H(c_2)|\bigr)$ satisfies $\rsd(x)\ge \tfrac12-\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose an integer $K>2/\varepsilon$ and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:mult}, a hypotenuse $c$ with
$h:=|H(c)|\ge K+3$. Take $c_1=c$, $c_2=5$, so $|H(c_2)|=2$ and $c\ne 5$ since $h\ge 3>2$. With
$m=2$ and $S=h+2$, Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} at the first cluster gives
\[
  \rsd(x)\;\ge\;\frac{h}{h+2}-\frac12 \;=\;\frac12-\frac{2}{h+2}.
\]
Since $h\ge K>2/\varepsilon$, we get $2/(h+2)<2/h<\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:half} is the strongest structural statement of the round: overdispersion in
clustered Pythagorean search is not a nuisance parameter that vanishes as the number of sampled
pairs grows. There is no universal averaging bound, because profiles built from genuine
hypotenuse clusters exist with floor arbitrarily close to $1/2$.

\section{Escaping the floor: inverse-variance pooling}\label{sec:pool}

\begin{definition}
For independent runs indexed by $s$ with variances $v_i>0$, the inverse-variance pooled variance
is $V(s,v)=\bigl(\sum_{i\in s} v_i^{-1}\bigr)^{-1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Pooling never hurts]
For every $j\in s$, $V(s,v)\le v_j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
All $v_i^{-1}$ are positive, so $v_j^{-1}\le\sum_i v_i^{-1}$; inverting reverses the inequality
on positives.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equal variances]\label{thm:eqvar}
If $v_i=\sigma^{2}>0$ for all $i\in s$ and $s\ne\emptyset$, then $V(s,v)=\sigma^{2}/|s|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sum_{i\in s}\sigma^{-2}=|s|\sigma^{-2}$; invert.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The named follow-up condition is achievable]
Three independent runs each with one-run standard error $0.025$ pool to a joint standard error
$\sqrt{0.025^{2}/3}\approx 0.01443 < 0.02$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:eqvar} with $|s|=3$ and $\sigma=0.025$, then $0.025^{2}/3<0.02^{2}$ and
monotonicity of the square root.
\end{proof}

The value $0.025$ is the recorded looser-cut half-width divided by $1.96$:
$(1.1016-1.0051)/2\approx 0.0483$ and $0.0483/1.96\approx 0.0246$. But
Theorem~\ref{thm:multi} adds a burden no pooling discharges: if the new seeds again disagree in
sign, pooling is not merely uninformative but illegitimate, because pairwise-incompatible
intervals falsify the coverage assumption that inverse-variance weighting rests on. Reopening
the gate requires \emph{explaining} the sign flip, not out-voting it.

\section{Audit item: display truncation is not evidence}\label{sec:trunc}

One alarm arose from a terminal display: a quantity equal to $3.38\times 10^{-5}$ printed with
five-decimal fixed formatting as \texttt{0.00003}, which appeared to lie outside its confidence
interval. Recomputation from persisted raw counts reproduced the value exactly.

\begin{definition}
Five-decimal truncation is $\tr_5(x)=\lfloor 10^{5}x\rfloor/10^{5}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Truncation can leave an interval]
There exist reals $lo < x < hi$ with $\tr_5(x) < lo$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Take $lo=0.000031$, $x=0.0000338$, $hi=0.000035$. Then $10^{5}x=3.38$, so
$\lfloor 10^{5}x\rfloor=3$ and $\tr_5(x)=0.00003<lo$.
\end{proof}

Hence ``the displayed value lies outside the interval'' carries no information about the
underlying value. Separately, an independent $4000$-replicate rebootstrap from the persisted raw
counts returned $[1.0540,\,1.2611]$ against the in-run stored $[1.0541,\,1.2686]$: agreement to
three decimals on the lower limit and within Monte-Carlo error on the upper.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Pooled ratio with cluster bootstrap interval.} Given counts $x,y$ and replicate count
$B$: compute $r=(\sum_i x_i)/(\sum_i y_i)$; for $b=1,\dots,B$ draw $f(1),\dots,f(m)$ i.i.d.\
uniform from $\{1,\dots,m\}$ and set
$r^{*}_b=\bigl(\sum_k x_{f(k)}\bigr)/\bigl(\sum_k y_{f(k)}\bigr)$; return $r$ and the empirical
$\alpha/2$, $1-\alpha/2$ quantiles. Cost $O(Bm)$. Resampling \emph{clusters} is exactly why the
floor of \S\ref{sec:floor} binds.

\paragraph{Exact relative dispersion and floor.} Compute $S=\sum_i x_i$, $\bar x = S/m$,
$\rsd = \sqrt{\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^{2}}/S$, and $\max_j x_j/S - 1/m$; assert the floor.
Cost $O(m)$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:isboot} this checks the reported interval itself.

\paragraph{Hypotenuse-multiplicity witness.} Given $k$, set
$C=\prod_{v=0}^{k-1}((v+2)^{2}+1)$; for each $v$ put $h=(v+2)^{2}+1$, $t=C/h$ and emit
$\bigl(((v+2)^{2}-1)t,\;2(v+2)t\bigr)$. Optionally count $|H(C)|$ exactly by factoring $C$ and
multiplying representation counts of its primes $\equiv 1\pmod 4$. Since
$\log C_k\sim 2k\log k$, exhaustive verification is feasible only for small $k$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\paragraph{Sharp multiplicity growth.} The construction provably delivers $k$ hits but
measurably delivers far more. The key insight is that the scaled family sees one primitive triple
per factor while the true count is multiplicative in the primes $\equiv 1\pmod 4$ dividing
$C_k$, so the gap between proved and true bound is itself a clean arithmetic function; the
existence result supplies a skeleton into which a Gaussian-integer factorisation count drops
without redoing the distinctness argument.

\paragraph{Distributional, not just worst-case, floor.} Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} is a
deterministic bound at a fixed profile; a future gate needs the \emph{typical} floor when
clusters are drawn from the hypotenuse distribution. The max/mean ratio of hypotenuse cluster
sizes grows like a divisor-type function, so the design effect should grow like a power of
$\log$ rather than stay bounded. The exact variance identity reduces any such statement to one
about $\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^{2}$ alone.

\paragraph{Sign flip as a formal falsification rule.} Theorem~\ref{thm:multi} gives the linear
degradation $s(1-\alpha)\le 1$. The natural strengthening is partial disagreement: a union bound
over sign-partitioned events converting ``how many runs flipped'' into ``how wrong the nominal
coverage is'', with rates interpolating between the fully-disjoint and fully-nested extremes.
The named three-seed follow-up will produce exactly such multi-run data.

\paragraph{Mediant rigidity and trimmed estimators.} Theorem~\ref{thm:envelope} makes the pooled
ratio a weighted median-like statistic whose sensitivity is governed entirely by the
largest-control clusters, suggesting a trimmed or winsorised pooled estimator with a provably
smaller floor: discard the top few clusters, pay a small bias, and buy a large reduction in
$\max_j x_j/S$. Quantifying that trade-off against Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} is a well-posed
optimisation.

\paragraph{Barrier framing.} The relevant frontier is scale smoothness in the band $u\ge 6$--$14$.
This round adds no breach, no new method, and shaves no constant. It adds a null with quantified
resolution floors --- a strengthening of the map rather than a step across it. The honesty arc is
complete: bank, downgrade, letter-of-rule null with independence audit, and finally clean
rejection by sign flip.

